WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.030
This article says a new AI model

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.590
has billions of weights.

3
00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.390
Nobody set one of them by hand.

4
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.140
So how did the network find them?

5
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.780
Today: how a network learns,

6
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.980
one small step at a time.

7
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.420
One neuron draws one straight line.

8
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.080
Put a few neurons side by side,

9
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.020
each drawing its own line.

10
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.350
A neuron in the next layer

11
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.710
combines their answers.

12
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.770
Two lines make a band; a few

13
00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.410
lines can fence in a circle.

14
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.290
So how does it learn?

15
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.700
At first its weights are

16
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.240
random, so it guesses.

17
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.460
Show it an example and compare its

18
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.020
answer with the right label.

19
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.980
The gap is the error: a number

20
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.660
for how wrong it was.

21
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.480
Then the clever part.

22
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.570
For every weight, the computer works

23
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.370
out whether a tiny nudge up would make

24
00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.950
the error bigger or smaller.

25
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.710
It nudges each weight the way

26
00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:02.870
that shrinks the error.

27
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.650
Then the next example, thousands of times.

28
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.330
That loop is training: guess,

29
00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.010
measure the error, nudge every

30
00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.450
weight a little downhill, and repeat.

31
00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.870
It’s called gradient descent.

32
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.850
Gradient means slope, and

33
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.510
descent means going down.

34
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.710
Picture walking down a hill in thick fog.

35
00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:30.630
You can’t see the valley, but you feel the

36
00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.810
slope, and step down, again and again.

37
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.110
Huge strides could carry you over the

38
00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.890
valley and up the other side.

39
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.280
Tiny steps are safe, but you’d

40
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.720
still be walking at nightfall.

41
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:45.400
Quick check!

42
00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.090
Your network’s error jumps up

43
00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.250
and down and never settles.

44
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.200
What would you try first?

45
00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:54.550
Smaller steps.

46
00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.130
A jumping error usually means the steps

47
00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.150
overshoot the bottom of the valley.

48
00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.530
In the lab, the step size

49
00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.770
is the learning rate.

50
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.040
Too small is slow; too big never settles.

51
00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.900
The examples matter too:

52
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.320
they make the hill.

53
00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.710
Train a leaf-disease spotter only on sunny

54
00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.510
photos, and it may fail on cloudy days.

55
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.190
With too few examples, it can

56
00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:22.850
learn a shape that isn’t there.

57
00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.230
Varied examples, from different phones,

58
00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.550
farms and light, teach the real shape.

59
00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.830
In the lab, you train a real

60
00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:33.990
network on your phone.

61
00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:35.990
Pick the circle with no

62
00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.770
hidden layers: it fails.

63
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.040
Add a layer of four neurons, press

64
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.180
Train, and watch the error fall as the

65
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.240
background bends around the circle.

66
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.670
Then try another learning rate.

67
00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:50.700
Your turn.

68
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.120
Which of these needs at

69
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.440
least one hidden layer?

70
00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.260
Circles inside a ring: no straight line

71
00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:02.460
can do it, so it needs a hidden layer.

72
00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.940
Remember: layers combine

73
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.420
lines into shapes.

74
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.900
Training repeats guess,

75
00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.160
error, nudge downhill.

76
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.700
And it needs sensible steps

77
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.260
and varied examples.

78
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:17.960
Try it in class tomorrow:

79
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.860
play “Warmer, colder”.

80
00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.430
One pupil steps out while the

81
00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:23.750
class hides the chalk.

82
00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.820
Back inside, they move only by

83
00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.900
“warmer” or “colder”.

84
00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.170
Then ask: what was the error,

85
00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:32.490
what were the steps?

86
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.960
That’s training, following

87
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:35.940
the error downhill.
